<EXPERIMENT_PACKAGE><EXPERIMENT accession="SRX22239377" alias="GSM7866518_r1"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRX22239377</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="GEO">GSM7866518_r1</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><TITLE>GSM7866518: PMA-stimulated THP1 cells, 1h, vehicle (DMSO:EtOH), RAR ChIP-seq, replicate 1; Homo sapiens; ChIP-Seq</TITLE><STUDY_REF accession="SRP468585"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP468585</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject">PRJNA1032483</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS></STUDY_REF><DESIGN><DESIGN_DESCRIPTION/><SAMPLE_DESCRIPTOR accession="SRS19293536"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS19293536</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="GEO">GSM7866518</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS></SAMPLE_DESCRIPTOR><LIBRARY_DESCRIPTOR><LIBRARY_NAME>GSM7866518</LIBRARY_NAME><LIBRARY_STRATEGY>ChIP-Seq</LIBRARY_STRATEGY><LIBRARY_SOURCE>GENOMIC</LIBRARY_SOURCE><LIBRARY_SELECTION>ChIP</LIBRARY_SELECTION><LIBRARY_LAYOUT><SINGLE/></LIBRARY_LAYOUT><LIBRARY_CONSTRUCTION_PROTOCOL>The cells were cross-linked with 2 mM disuccinimidyl glutarate for 40 minutes and 1% methanol-free formaldehyde for 10 minutes. After quenching and cell lysis, chromatin was sheared using a Diagenode Bioruptor sonicator. Precipitated and washed samples were purified using NucleoSpin Gel and PCR Clean-up Kit (Macherey-Nagel). Indexed libraries were prepared from ChIP-DNA using a TruSeq ChIP Sample Preparation Kit (Illumina).</LIBRARY_CONSTRUCTION_PROTOCOL></LIBRARY_DESCRIPTOR></DESIGN><PLATFORM><ILLUMINA><INSTRUMENT_MODEL>NextSeq 500</INSTRUMENT_MODEL></ILLUMINA></PLATFORM></EXPERIMENT><SUBMISSION lab_name="" center_name="UNIVERSITY OF DEBRECEN" accession="SRA1740180" alias="SUB13930467"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRA1740180</PRIMARY_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="UNIVERSITY OF DEBRECEN">SUB13930467</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS></SUBMISSION><Organization type="institute"><Name>University of Debrecen</Name><Contact email="lnagy@jhmi.edu"><Address><Street>Egyetem ter 1.</Street><City>Debrecen</City><Country>Hungary</Country></Address><Name><First>Laszlo</First><Last>Nagy</Last></Name></Contact></Organization><STUDY center_name="GEO" alias="GSE246308" accession="SRP468585"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP468585</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject" label="primary">PRJNA1032483</EXTERNAL_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="GEO">GSE246308</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><DESCRIPTOR><STUDY_TITLE>Genomic regions occupied by both RARa and VDR are involved in the convergence and cooperation of retinoid and vitamin D signaling pathways.</STUDY_TITLE><STUDY_TYPE existing_study_type="Other"/><STUDY_ABSTRACT>In this study, the retinoid and vitamin D pathways were investigated in PMA-differentiated THP-1 cells treated with or without the RARa agonist, AM580, and/or the VDR agonist, 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3 (1,25-vitD or calcitriol), using global approaches. Binding regions of RARa and VDR were determined in PMA-differentiated THP-1 cells using chromatin immunoprecipitation coupled with high-throughput sequencing (ChIP-seq). The gene sets regulated by AM580 and/or 1,25-vitD were identified by RNA-sequencing (RNA-seq). The activity of the regulatory regions was determined by MED1 (a subunit of the Mediator complex) ChIP-seq and chromatin openness was measured using assay for transposase-accessible chromatin with sequencing (ATAC-seq).  Overall design: THP1 cells were stimulated with 20 nM PMA for 16 hours. In the case of RARa and VDR ChIP-seq samples: PMA-differentiated THP-1 cells were treated with vehicle (DMSO:EtOH), RARa agonist (100 nM AM580) or VDR agonist (100 nM calcitriol or 1,25-vitD) for 1 hour. Two replicates of each condition were analyzed by ChIP-seq. In the case of MED1 ChIP-seq samples: PMA-differentiated THP-1 cells were treated with vehicle (DMSO:EtOH), RARa agonist (100 nM AM580) and/or VDR agonist (100 nM calcitriol or 1,25-vitD) for 2 hour. Three replicates of each condition were analyzed by ChIP-seq.</STUDY_ABSTRACT><CENTER_PROJECT_NAME>GSE246308</CENTER_PROJECT_NAME></DESCRIPTOR><STUDY_LINKS>